title Clear cadence bounds the footprint -- the window caveat run as a simulation
prompt The baton and the diffuser inner prompt, read as one lap: take the next self-generated fruit the inner prompt leaves open, and write it at Field.
think Read recursion-prompts/diffuser-inner.md. Its newest fruit (20261009.160141) left one caveat standing: the mixed-lifetime reading covers the no-clear case only, and clear() is the one release gesture Region has.
think Ran a scratch probe in .lap/clear/: the same 90/10 workload, with clear() every 100, 1,000 and 10,000 steps. Each clear re-copies the live set, which is the batch-then-clear pattern real callers use.
think The "never clear" row reproduces 958.56 exactly, so the probe walks the same random stream as the prior paper. That is the cross-check that the new code is the old model plus one change.
think A simple projection, live plus one window's replacement bytes, lands within about five percent of the measured ratio at 1,000 steps on both mixes. The formula is stated with its horizon, assumptions, and falsifier.
think The clear cadence lowers the footprint ratio but moves the cost to copy traffic, 1,486 bytes per step at a 100-step cadence on the log mix. The paper names that price rather than hiding it.
think Wrote the essay at active-designing/date/20261009/20261009-160733_the-clear-cadence-bounds-the-footprint.md, graded it with the report card (B+ 89 at Field), and deleted the scratch room.
obs The simulation is bytes only. Allocator metadata, alignment, and time are unmeasured, so nothing here is a claim about Region on metal.
obs The next falsifier, a byte-threshold clear, is named and not run. Running it would be a second fruit, and the lap already has one landed.
obs No witness, no module, no code moved. The only changes are the essay, the inner prompt's fruit and revision line, and this log with its day-shelf row.
